As I continued to build out the features, the code structure started to get disjoined and I wanted to note down some changes I need to make:

- I need to group the components into the modes more cleanly. 
    - The design and implementation components are in their own folder but the implementation is in the root. This is mainly because I have actually defined more about the application strucutre as I was exploring and I didn't take the time to make changes to the folder structure to reflect this.

- I need to separate the stores into slices in a cleaner way
    - Right now when I select the behavior in the behavioral control graph in the design mode, it is using the thunk in the root store. Then when I deselect a behavior, I am manually clearing the transform output log, the primitive. If I create a separate selector for the design mode, I can cleanly manage the state in the design thunk and simplify my life. 

- I need to url arguments for sepecific traces or behaviors and then I need to add keybaord shortcuts for moving between the modes.
    - The reason for the traces or behavior url args is because it will allow me to load the UI into a sepcific state.
    - Moving between nodes is because it will help me see the results of my executions.

- Add behavioral control graph to the debug view and allow a visualization of which node is being inspected using the position from the design trace.
    - This one is relavtivel simply, I just want to visualize where I am in the atomic design path on top of the behavioral control graph.

- All the toolbars should share the same style sheet.
    - This is part of a bigger change that I mentioned a while back where I am defining style sheets on the fly and this one will probably wait until much later when I create my component library. 

Essentially, since I established these three modes, I haven't taken the time to make changes to the code structure to reflect these changes. SO I am leaving a note here for myself to fix it and to be consistent with my naming for the design, implementation and debugging modes.

Luckily, none of these changes are very difficult and once I dedicate a day to this, I will be able to move it forward cleanly.

Note: One note I wanted to make was, when I save a design file that has a lot of traces, the websocket message becomes too large and causes an error. In the future I will have intelligent saving mechanisms where only the modified data will be sent to the server to be saved. Right now I send the whole file, I can either limit the number of active traces to a fixed amount or I can send a multi part message through the websocket. However, this isn't priority because it doesn't affect functionality, I will address it soon when it becomes more of a concern.